  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms today as a \nstrong cold front impacts the forecast area. \n\nA potent surface low pressure system tracks east from the Great \nLakes region towards New England today with the associated cold \nfront pushing across the forecast area later today. This strong cold \nfront, combined with a deep upper level trough digging across \neastern CONUS, leads to an increased chance of scattered severe \nthunderstorms this afternoon and into the evening. Showers and \nthunderstorms increase in coverage between 2PM and 8PM with storms \nlingering later in the southeastern portions of the forecast area. \nMultiple bowing and linear segments are possible with damaging wind \ngusts as the primary hazard type. That being said, cooling mid-level \ntemperatures contributes to a threat of large hail and a brief \ntornado is possible due to low-level shear. The Storm Prediction \nCenter has the majority of the forecast area (with the exception of \nwestern Maryland being in a marginal risk) in a slight risk for \nsevere weather. \n\nIn addition to the severe weather threat, a moist airmass with PWATS \nexceeding 1.7 inches makes strong thunderstorms capable of producing \ntorrential downpours. Where PWATS and ensemble QPF \nprobabilities are highest, a Flood Watch is in effect from 8AM \nthis morning to 2AM early Wednesday morning in northeast \nMaryland. The most vulnerable locations would be urbanized areas\nalong I-95 as well as areas which have experienced heavy \nrainfall in recent days. Recent HREF probabilities have slightly\nshifted the bullseye of QPF maxima eastward, so kept the Flood \nWatch northeast of the DC metros although an expansion may be \nneeded later should future guidance indicate an increased risk. \n\nIn the wake of the frontal system, surface high pressure builds over \nthe area from the west with slightly cooler and drier conditions \nexpected Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few chances for rain through the weekend, but no \n hazardous weather expected between Wednesday and Sunday at this\n time.\n\nThe upper-low over the northeast CONUS will linger through Friday \nbefore finally pulling off to the northeast. As a result, there will \nbe non-zero rain chances each day, primarily areas further north and \neast. Friday could be dry for all, but still a bit of uncertainty \nregarding exactly when the upper low departs. \n\nA broad upper-level trough will begin to form over the Ohio River \nValley over the weekend. As this trough tracks east, cloud cover & \nprecipitation chances will begin to increase, particularly between \nSaturday evening and Sunday as the low tracks offshore and towards \nthe northeast. Severe probabilities look low at this time, with a \npretty weak wind field aloft. Nonetheless, weekend showers are \npossible with this system should the synoptic setup continue to \ntrend toward development over the Midwest.",
